## 1. Executive Summary
This report analyzes a malicious PE32 x86 binary (sha256: 7fbde4a47c916e4e3bbbb8c0e77d947216452f1f30e7b27f9e68a7642c8f72a6) masquerading as a legitimate Tencent GameLoop Android emulator installer. The sample received a malicious verdict with a confidence score of 90, attributed to multi-functional info-stealing and remote access capabilities consistent with DarkGate, LucaStealer, Remcos, and related malware families (source: llm_judge, verdict). The sample is signed with an expired DigiCert code signing certificate (valid 2020-11-25 to 2024-02-22, expired prior to the 2026-07-03 collection date) (source: malcat, static_profile, Certificate::Validity), a common indicator of trojanized legitimate software. Static analysis reveals high-signal behavioral indicators including process injection (T1055), anti-debugging (T1622), anti-VM evasion (T1497.001), download capability (T1105), HTTP C2 communication (T1071.001), registry persistence (T1112), and keylogging (T1056.001), confirmed via PE import analysis, capa rule matches, and YARA signatures. While the sample exhibits heavy obfuscation (high entropy, control flow flattening, XOR loops, API hashing), these are neutral protection signals; the observed behavioral intent confirms malicious classification.

